From: nix-wasm <eric@codebutler.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Jul 2026 00:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] virtio_wasm: register a virtio-snd device (VIRTIO_ID_SOUND)

Register a virtio-snd device on the wasm virtio transport so the stock
mainline virtio-snd driver (sound/virtio/, CONFIG_SND_VIRTIO) gives the
guest a sound card (issue #145): /dev/snd/* appears, ALSA userspace
(alsa-lib, libcanberra event sounds) works unmodified, and the host
receives the PCM stream through runtime/virtio/snd-device.js — the same
"everything is a virtio device" pattern as console/blk/9p/vsock (#10).

Device index: pinned to host index 6 with an explicit `= 6` — the
previously-unused slot between the 9P channels (4,5) and vsock (7). The
explicit value keeps the index stable regardless of which CONFIG-guarded
earlier slots are built; the host side (runtime/kernel-worker.js /
runtime/kernel-host.js VW_DEV_SND = 6) and the kernel must agree on the
index that selects the device in every host-call. Guarded by
IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SND_VIRTIO) so the enum slot and the init call are a
no-op when the driver is not built.

Mainline virtio_snd.c binds four virtqueues — controlq (q0), eventq
(q1), txq (q2), rxq (q3) — within the transport's VIRTIO_WASM_MAX_VQS=8,
and reads the jack/stream/chmap counts from config space (struct
virtio_snd_config). The host serves { jacks=0, streams=1, chmaps=0 } and
answers the PCM control requests + consumes tx periods per the virtio
spec §5.14 in runtime/virtio/snd-device.js: one s16 48kHz playback
stream, capture out of scope. The kernel side here is just the device
registration — no custom sound code, the stock driver does the rest.

The default 64-entry virtqueue is left unchanged: an ALSA period is one
3-descriptor chain ([virtio_snd_pcm_xfer][data][virtio_snd_pcm_status])
and the driver posts at most buffer_bytes/period_bytes messages at once
(a handful), far under 64.

Part of issue #145 (guest audio: virtio-snd device + alsa-lib).
---
 drivers/virtio/virtio_wasm.c | 22 ++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/virtio/virtio_wasm.c b/drivers/virtio/virtio_wasm.c
--- a/drivers/virtio/virtio_wasm.c
+++ b/drivers/virtio/virtio_wasm.c
@@ -434,6 +434,15 @@
 	VW_DEV_9P_ROOT,		/* virtio-9p: pc VFS export (mount tag "pcroot") */
 	VW_DEV_9P_NIXCACHE,	/* virtio-9p: nix binary cache (tag "nixcache") */
 #endif
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SND_VIRTIO)
+	/*
+	 * virtio-snd: the guest's sound card (issue #145). Pinned to host index
+	 * 6 with an explicit value (the previously-unused slot between the 9P
+	 * channels and vsock); the host (VW_DEV_SND = 6 in kernel-worker.js /
+	 * kernel-host.js) must agree on this index.
+	 */
+	VW_DEV_SND = 6,
+#endif
 #if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_VIRTIO_VSOCKETS)
 	/*
 	 * virtio-vsock: AF_VSOCK socket channel for the /Ctl bridge (issue #10
@@ -530,6 +539,18 @@
 		pr_warn("virtio_wasm: vsock register failed: %ld\n", PTR_ERR(vw));
 #endif
 
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_SND_VIRTIO)
+	/*
+	 * virtio-snd sound card (stock sound/virtio driver, issue #145). The
+	 * host serves the jack/stream/chmap counts in config space and drives
+	 * the control/event/tx/rx vqs (runtime/virtio/snd-device.js): one s16
+	 * 48kHz playback stream, no capture.
+	 */
+	vw = virtio_wasm_register(VW_DEV_SND, VIRTIO_ID_SOUND);
+	if (IS_ERR(vw))
+		pr_warn("virtio_wasm: snd register failed: %ld\n", PTR_ERR(vw));
+#endif
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
